    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1330">

                <pb facs="1426" n="1412" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg1412"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">103.9169/4034: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stewart</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Managua</hi></placeName>, [<date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                            notAfter="1944-06-14T01:10:00-06:00"
                            notBefore="1944-06-13T00:00:00-06:00">undated</date>].<lb/>[Received
                        June 14, 1944—3:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>362. Regarding decentralization rollback to a minimum positive list, reference is
                    made to the Department’s circular airgram dated May 25, 11:15 a.m.<anchor n="39"
                        xml:id="d1330-f1"/> and Department’s A–127 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >197</hi>], June 6, 5 [<hi rendition="#italic">5:05</hi>] p.m. The Nicaraguan
                    Country Agency has accepted the decentralization rollback and will discontinue
                    June 30, 1944, requiring import permits for commodities that are not covered by
                    import recommendations which will then be needed for the positive list only.
                    Exchange control by the Nicaraguan Government will, however, continue for the
                    time being at least since they state they find it necessary. In lieu of import
                    permits, the importer will after July 1 submit his orders with the same number
                    of copies as in the case of import permits to a one-man Nicaraguan Exchange
                    Control Commission for exchange authorization, the disposition of copies
                    remaining the same. Major Thomas Downing<anchor n="40" xml:id="d1330-f2"/> and
                    in his temporary absence, Colonel Irving Lindberg,<anchor n="41"
                        xml:id="d1330-f3"/> both Americans, will act as the Exchange Control
                    Commission.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stewart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1330-f1" n="39" xml:id="d1330-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1330-f2" n="40" xml:id="d1330-f2-content">
                <p>Major Downing was President of the Nicaraguan Junta de Control de Precios and
                    Assistant Collector of Customs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1330-f3" n="41" xml:id="d1330-f3-content">
                <p>Colonel Lindberg was Collector General of Customs and High Commissioner in
                    Nicaragua. For correspondence on the duties of Mr. Downing and Mr. Lindberg, see
                        <ref target="#pg1396">pp. 1396</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
